**THEOREM 3.5.** An elementwise function
$$f : |D_0 \times D_1| \to |D_2|$$
of two arguments comes from an approximable mapping iff for each fixed $a \in |D_0|$ and each fixed $b \in |D_1|$ the transformations
$$x \mapsto f(x, b) \quad \text{and} \quad y \mapsto f(a, y)$$
come from approximable mappings of one argument.

*Proof:* As this is the first time we have had to deal with constants in functions, a lemma is useful.

The product ↔ binary approximable-map correspondence is with the
[approximable maps of two arguments](../MappingsBetweenDomains/1980_ApproximableMap2.md) peer.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`theorem_3_5`).** Joint approximable maps out of a product correspond bijectively to binary approximable maps, and the correspondence agrees elementwise with pairing.

*Proof.* Assemble the product–binary equivalence with the elementwise pairing law. ∎
